Files in this folder are based on MariaDB Connector/C 3.4.11.

Original source: https://github.com/mariadb-corporation/mariadb-connector-c,
tag v3.4.11 (commit be67a4fc1e0493913732df90e562f122bff9dfe3). Licensed
under the GNU LGPL 2.1 or later (COPYING.LIB).

It replaces MySQL Connector/C 6.0.0, the client library of the MySQL
database driver (revdb's dbmysql) until OXT-Beyond 0.2.8. MariaDB
Connector/C speaks the protocol of MySQL servers as well as MariaDB's,
including MySQL 8's caching_sha2_password login, and keeps the libmysql
API (include/mysql.h) that the driver calls.

Only the client library is kept: libmariadb/, the authentication, socket,
named pipe, shared memory and zlib plugins under plugins/, the public and
private headers in include/, and COPYING.LIB. The tests, the unittest
plugins, the other TLS back ends (GnuTLS, Schannel), the dialog, PAM, GSSAPI
and clear-password plugins, the trace and replication plugins and the CMake
files are left out.

include/mariadb_version.h and libmariadb/ma_client_plugin.c are what CMake
made from their .in files for this build; include/ma_config.h is written
for every platform the engine is built for (it says from what), since the
engine builds the library with gyp (libmysql.gyp) and runs no CMake.

Local changes, marked "OXT-Beyond" in the sources:

- include/ma_config.h: the connection's character set, when a script names
  none, is latin1, as with Connector/C 6.0 (the library's own default is
  utf8mb4), so that the text a server sends back is unchanged; and
  DEFAULT_SSL_VERIFY_SERVER_CERT is left undefined, so that a connection
  uses TLS only when a script asks for it, as before.
- libmariadb/ma_client_plugin.c: the plugins built into the library are
  native, old (mysql_old_password, which 6.0 allowed and so is not
  disabled), ed25519, caching_sha2_password, sha256_password, socket, zlib,
  and on Windows named pipe and shared memory. A plugin is never loaded
  from a file (mysql_load_plugin_v refuses).
- libmariadb/secure/oxt_crypt.c and include/ma_crypt.h: SHA-1 and the
  SHA-2 hashes that the logins use are the library's own code instead of
  OpenSSL's. The engine reaches OpenSSL through stubs that work only once
  it has loaded its SSL library, and a login needs the hashes whether or
  not the connection uses TLS. ma_oxt_openssl_loaded, which the driver sets
  once the SSL library is loaded, says whether OpenSSL may be called.
- plugins/auth/caching_sha2_pw.c and plugins/auth/sha256_pw.c: without
  TLS these logins send the password encrypted with the server's RSA key,
  which needs OpenSSL; with no SSL library loaded they fail with an error
  saying so instead of calling it.
- libmariadb/mariadb_lib.c: mysql_server_init starts OpenSSL only when
  the SSL library is loaded; otherwise the first connection with TLS
  starts it.
- plugins/auth/caching_sha2_pw.c: a connection counts as encrypted, so
  that the password may go as it is, only when it has TLS, not when TLS
  was asked for (a server without TLS is connected to without it).

TLS is OpenSSL's (libmariadb/secure/openssl.c), through the engine's
OpenSSL stubs (thirdparty/libopenssl/ssl.stubs).
